@meta
id: 24-15
title: Paste the word under the cursor
chapter: 24
type: lesson
skills: cmdline-edit
par: 32
minutes: 5
vscode: double-click the identifier, Ctrl-C, Ctrl-H, Ctrl-V into the replace box
open: payment.service.ts

@brief
`refundLegacy` is gone from the gateway package. Its replacement is
already imported on line 1, and every call in this file has to use it
instead. Three calls, same name each time.

The replacement is twenty characters long and you are going to type it
zero times. The command line can read from the file. `Ctrl-R` followed
by a register name pastes that register into the command you are
typing; `Ctrl-R` followed by `Ctrl-W` pastes the word under the cursor.
Put the cursor on the imported name and write the substitute with the
new name pasted in.

@keys
| `Ctrl-R` `Ctrl-W` | on the command line: insert the word under the cursor |
| `Ctrl-R` `a` | on the command line: insert register `a` |
| `Ctrl-R` `/` | on the command line: insert the last search pattern |
| `2w` | from `import` onto the identifier |

@hints
Get the cursor onto `refundThroughGateway` in the import first. The
word under the cursor is whatever the cursor is on when you press `:`.
--
The pattern side is typed; it is short. The replacement side is
`Ctrl-R` then `Ctrl-W`, and the word appears as if you had typed it.
--
`2w` then `:%s/refundLegacy/` `Ctrl-R` `Ctrl-W` `/g` and Enter.

@solution
    2w  :%s/refundLegacy/<C-r><C-w>/g<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-eight keystrokes, two of which stand in for twenty.

`Ctrl-R` on the command line is the same `Ctrl-R` as in insert mode from
chapter 3: it pastes a register. The command line has a few extra
registers that only make sense there. `Ctrl-W` is the word under the
cursor, `Ctrl-A` the WORD, `Ctrl-L` the whole line. `/` is the last
search, `:` is the last command line, `"` is the unnamed register.
`Ctrl-R` `/` is how you get the search you just did into a substitute
when you want to edit it rather than reuse it blind.

Chapter 14's `*` then `:%s//new/` covers the case where the word under
the cursor is the *pattern*. This covers the case where it is the
*replacement*, or where it is going into a command that has no pattern
at all: `:vimgrep`, `:e`, `:b`, `:h`. `:h` `Ctrl-R` `Ctrl-W` is how you
look up whatever you are staring at.

@start payment.service.ts
import { refundThroughGateway } from '../gateway/refund';
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';

@injectable()
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string, amount: number) {
    return refundLegacy(orderId, amount);
  }

  public async refundPartial(orderId: string, amount: number) {
    return refundLegacy(orderId, amount);
  }

  public async refundAll(orderIds: string[], amount: number) {
    return Promise.all(orderIds.map((orderId) => refundLegacy(orderId, amount)));
  }
}

@expected payment.service.ts
import { refundThroughGateway } from '../gateway/refund';
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';

@injectable()
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string, amount: number) {
    return refundThroughGateway(orderId, amount);
  }

  public async refundPartial(orderId: string, amount: number) {
    return refundThroughGateway(orderId, amount);
  }

  public async refundAll(orderIds: string[], amount: number) {
    return Promise.all(orderIds.map((orderId) => refundThroughGateway(orderId, amount)));
  }
}
